==============================================================================
NOTE: For Classes, specialization is explicit. For functions, it is implicit.

So template <> class X <int, float, string> { .. };
But never template <> void func<int, float, string>( ... ) { .. }
The latter is deduced.
==============================================================================

Class
=====
[a] Original

template <typename T, typename U, typename V>
class X {
};

[b] Partial

template <typename V, typename U>
class X<int, U, V> {
};


[c] Full
template <>
class X <int, int, float> {
};

Make sure the empty template <> is maintained

====================================================================================================
[Update] Consider this format
template <typename T, typename U>  // [1]
struct X <...> {                   // [2]

}

Here [1] is where you declare all the "unknown" types. [2] is where you declare all the known and unknown types

If all types are unknown (meaning no specialization has happened) then [2] is empty!!
