Function
========
[a] Original

template <typename T, typename U, typename V>
void func(T a, U b) { }


[b] Partial

template <typename V>
void func(int a, float b) { }


[c] Full

template <string>
void func(int a, float b) { }


So the key here is you never explicitly write what the specialized types are. So never do void func<int, int, float>.
That is deduced. 

Notice in [c] we did a Full Specialization by providing template <string> which is a Type Parameter that is not actively
used. Its done thus and not template <> void func<int>(int a, float b) { }


UPDATE: You can explicitly specialize by providing the type inside the template declaration like this
template <int, int, int> 
void function(int a, int b, int c)
     
        Thats fine

Course you just don't explicitly specialize thus 
template <>
void function<int, int, int>(int a, int b, int c)

The latter style is done for Class Type specialization. 
